--TEST--
A block groups statements and opens no scope, so a local declared inside one is the same local after the closing brace; what the block does affect is definite assignment, and a declaration in a loop body is one statement that runs on every round — `rule:types/declaration`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/expression-statements-blocks-and-declarations
// A block `{ … }` groups statements and opens no scope `rule:types/declaration`,
// so both names below are the same function-scoped locals after their braces
// close, at any depth.
{
    int $inner = 40;
    {
        int $deeper = 2;
    }
}
echo $inner + $deeper, "\n";

// What a block does affect is definite assignment: a declaration with no
// initializer is readable once every path has assigned it, and a block that
// always runs is such a path.
int $late;
bool $flag = true;
if ($flag) {
    $late = 1;
} else {
    $late = 2;
}
echo $late, "\n";

// "Once" counts declaration *statements*, not executions: the declaration
// below is one statement, and it initialises `$label` afresh on every round.
int $round = 0;
while ($round < 3) {
    string $label = "round " . ($round as string);
    echo $label, "\n";
    $round = $round + 1;
}

// `;` on its own is an empty statement.
;
echo "done\n";
--EXPECT--
42
1
round 0
round 1
round 2
done
